---
import Notebook from "../layouts/Notebook.astro";
import { CLUB, REEL, reelSrcset } from "../data/people";
import { WEEKS } from "../data/schedule";

// What the club is building this year. Source: the qBronco Project Plan,
// updated Oct 7, 2026, with the Oct 14 changes. The three checkpoints come from
// the schedule data, so they always match the schedule page's footnotes.

const checkpoints = WEEKS.filter((w) => w.about);

const github = CLUB.links.find((l) => l.name === "GitHub")!;

// one print from the Oct 7 Bell state lab beside each team. the photos and
// their alt text live with the reel in src/data/people.ts.
const TEAMS = [
  {
    name: "systems",
    does: "builds the processor in Verilog, then puts it on the Nexys A7 board.",
    photo: "bell-lab-qubis",
    caption: "lab 1, Bell state in hand",
    tilt: -1.6,
  },
  {
    name: "programming",
    does: "writes the compiler that turns a Qiskit circuit into our instructions, the python model of the processor that defines what the hardware is supposed to do, and the checker that compares results against Qiskit.",
    photo: "bell-lab-qiskit",
    caption: "the circuit in Qiskit",
    tilt: 1.2,
  },
  {
    name: "theory",
    does: "picks the gate set and the four programs, works out the expected answers on paper, and writes the paper.",
    photo: "bell-lab-math",
    caption: "the math, on the board",
    tilt: -0.9,
  },
].map((team) => ({ ...team, alt: REEL.find((p) => p.photo === team.photo)!.alt }));
---

<Notebook
  page="project"
  title="The project · QBronco (Quantum Broncos)"
  description="This year QBronco (the Quantum Broncos) builds a 3-qubit processor with its own instruction set, written in Verilog, run on an FPGA and checked against Qiskit. Three teams, no experience needed."
  scrawl="~ 2026–27"
  turn={{ label: "back to the front", href: "/" }}
>
  <header class="page-head">
    <div class="title-head">
      <h1 class="page-title">the project</h1>
      <svg class="title-underline" viewBox="0 0 320 26" preserveAspectRatio="none" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" stroke="#c2362f" stroke-width="2.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" d="M6 9 Q80 3 160 7 Q240 11 314 5"/><path vector-effect="non-scaling-stroke" d="M10 18 Q84 13 158 16 Q236 19 308 14"/></svg>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section>
    <!-- tiny H-gate circuit in the margin -->
    <svg class="margin-doodle circuit" viewBox="0 0 120 56" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" aria-hidden="true" fill="none" stroke="#5a5860" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M3 28.5 Q13 27.5 22 28.2"/><path d="M51 28 Q62 28.8 73 28.1"/><path d="M101 28.3 Q108 27.8 117 28.5"/><path d="M23.5 16.5 Q22.8 28 23.8 40.5 Q35.5 41.2 49.5 40 Q50.2 28.5 49 16.8 Q36 15.7 23.5 16.5"/><path d="M30 35 Q30.5 23.5 30 21.5"/><path d="M43 35 Q42.5 24 43 21"/><path d="M30 28.2 Q36.5 28.8 43 28.3"/><path d="M74.5 16 Q73.8 28 74.8 41 Q87 41.5 100 40.3 Q100.8 28.5 99.8 16.3 Q87 15.4 74.5 16"/><path d="M80.5 34.5 Q87 23.5 95 34"/><path d="M87.2 33.5 L93.5 23.5"/><circle cx="87.2" cy="33.8" r="1.3" fill="#5a5860" stroke="none"/></svg>

    <p>
      one build, all year, fall and spring. every wednesday, teams sit together
      and build, and if a team needs an idea, someone teaches it at the
      whiteboard. this year the build is our own 3-qubit processor. here's the
      whole thing:
    </p>

    <ul class="todo flow">
      <li>
        <strong>what we're building.</strong> a 3-qubit processor with its own
        16-bit instruction set, written in Verilog and run on an FPGA board (a
        Digilent Nexys A7). the design is modeled on eQASM, a published quantum
        instruction set from TU Delft, the way a CPU class models its chip on
        MIPS.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>what runs on it.</strong> four programs: a Bell state, quantum
        teleportation, a quantum Fourier transform, and Grover's search over 8
        items.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>how we know it works.</strong> a python model of the processor
        says what the hardware is supposed to do, and Qiskit is the answer key.
        every program has to match it.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>the headline.</strong> guess at random and you find 1 of 8
        items 12.5% of the time. our processor runs Grover's search and finds
        it with probability 121/128, about 94.5%.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="tilt-a">one target, three forms</h2>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li><strong>a python model of the processor.</strong> <span class="soft">fall.</span></li>
      <li><strong>the Verilog design, running in simulation.</strong> <span class="soft">fall into spring.</span></li>
      <li><strong>the design on the board.</strong> <span class="soft">spring.</span></li>
    </ol>
    <p class="soft">
      none of these is a real quantum computer. the FPGA is normal hardware
      doing the quantum math.
    </p>
    <p class="soft">
      you'll still see the Qubis around, the handheld model qubits you tap,
      bump and shake. they come out at some meetings, at recruiting events and
      at the Qubi workshops, but they're a teaching toy, not part of the build.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="tilt-d">three teams</h2>
    <ul class="subteams">
      {
        TEAMS.map((team) => (
          <li class="subteam">
            <figure class="polaroid polaroid-sm" style={`--tilt: ${team.tilt}deg`}>
              <img
                src={`/reel/${team.photo}-480.webp`}
                srcset={reelSrcset(team.photo)}
                sizes="(max-width: 640px) 173px, 187px"
                alt={team.alt}
                width="640"
                height="480"
                loading="lazy"
                decoding="async"
              />
              <figcaption class="polaroid-caption">{team.caption}</figcaption>
            </figure>
            <p>
              <strong>{team.name}.</strong> {team.does}
            </p>
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
    <p>
      each team picks its own lead, and you can join any of them with zero
      experience, even partway through the year: we'll give you the material
      to get caught up. the fall slide decks live on
      <a class="pen-blue" href={github.href} target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">the GitHub repo</a>
      too, so you can read at your own pace.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 class="tilt-b">three checkpoints</h2>
    <ul class="todo">
      {
        checkpoints.map((week) => (
          <li>
            <strong>
              {week.title}, {week.label}.
            </strong>{" "}
            {week.about}
          </li>
        ))
      }
    </ul>
    <p class="soft">spring dates can still move.</p>
    <p><a class="pen-blue" href="/schedule">every date is on the schedule &rarr;</a></p>
  </section>
</Notebook>
